        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Most of the air leakage was coming from the crawlspace. Properly insulating and air sealing this area will reduce the amount of heatloss and air leakage.



The bathroom fan is letting alot of cold air into the home. This should be assessed.



There are a lot of sopts around all the windows and doors that are very leaky.



See the attached thermal images to confirm these areas.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The problem with polyiso insulation that is on the exterior of the home is that, though it has the highest r-value of most insulation products, it loses about 40% of that r-value when it gets cold. It is tested at 25 degrees celcius and at that temperature the r-vaule is r- 6.5-7 per inch. At -25 when you need it most, you would get at most r-3 or 4 per inch. 



It is also a material that is considered a vapour retarder/barrier. If this insulation was taped at the seams, you may have moisture issues in your walls in a few years. What will happen is that the air in the wall gets trapped between the polyiso and the 6 mil poly on the interior of the wall. Since there is now a vapour barrier on both sides of the wall, the wall is not being properly ventilated.



When air is warmer, it is able to hold more amounts of moisture, which is a measure of relative humidity. The colder it gets, the less amount of moisture the air can hold. So when the warm air from inside the house meets the cold air from the polyiso, the amount of moisture in the warm air is still the same. With a cooler temperature in the wall, the relative humidity of the air has now increased and is now going to condense into a liquid (same as windows in the winter). This cool moist air is now trapped in the wall because of the polyiso, this is where mold and mildew can form, causing you a lot of problems and money. 



A way of avoiding this is by either adding a lot more polyiso on the outside to keep the wall warmer, or to replace the polyiso with a type two expanded polystyrene insulation (not a vapor retarder). Some contractors are also using semi-rigid insulation such as roxul on the exterior now. This could be an option for you.



The thermal bridging in the home can verify that the polyiso loses r-value in the winter. 1.5" of exterior insulation would usually help reduce this problem.



This is something that you should consider seriously.



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The ceiling is currently around R-30. The problem is that you are probably not getting the same amount of insulation value along the front and back of the house because of the small heel height of the trusses. The best way to insulated this small area would be to spray foam around the edges and then fill with insulation elsewhere.</ceilings>
                <foundation>Add 3.5 " of spray foam on the walls of the crawl space and the floor of the foundation</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>Since you have plans on tightening up the home. You should consider having a proper ventilation system installed. I would recommend installing a HRV. With older houses being so leaky, they were able the breathe. Now that you are tightening the home up, it still needs to "breathe" even though it can't as well as it used too. Having a HRV installed would provide you with the proper amount air to be exchanged in and out of the home. This will eliminate air quality issues and will help reduce the amount of moisture in the home. You could run into major issues down the road if you do not have a proper ventilation system.</VentilationSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
